{
  "dom_id": "step-q105335",
  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "Find and categorize all the local extrema of the function \n$f(x,y)$ \ngiven that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}4-4y-4x & 4-4x+{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}4-4y-4x & 4-4x+{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}],$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=4-4y-4x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=4-4x+{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4-4y-4x=0 \\\\ 4-4x+{y}^{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n4-4y-4x=0\\,⟹\\,x=1-y.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4-4(1-y)+{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}+4y & =0 \\\\ y(y+4) & =0 \\\\ y & =-4,0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBack-substituting these values into $x=1-y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\ny=-4\\,⟹\\,x=1-(-4)=5,\\,\\,y=0\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,⟹\\,x=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nHence, $(1,0)$ and $(5,-4)$ are stationary points of $f(x,y).$\n\nTo classify these stationary points, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4-4y-4x)=-4\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4-4x+{y}^{2})=-4 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4-4y-4x)=-4\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4-4x+{y}^{2})=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 2y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the second derivative at each of the stationary points and use it to categorize them:\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(1,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,0)=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(1,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(1,0)=det({f}^{″}(1,0))=(-4)\\cdot 0-(-4)^{2}=-16<0,\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(1,0)$ is a saddle point.\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(5,-4),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(5,-4)=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -4 \\\\ -4 & -8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(5,-4),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(5,-4)=det({f}^{″}(5,-4))=(-4)\\cdot (-8)-(-4)^{2}=16>0\n$$\n\nFinally, since $H(5,-4)>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(5,-4)={h}_{11}=-4<0,$ the function $f$ has a local maximum at $(5,-4).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(1,0)$ and a local maximum at $(5,-4).$",
